-Fantastic.

64
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:35,233
A little mouse should live in there.

65
00:03:35,366 --> 00:03:37,133
[giggling]

66
00:03:37,483 --> 00:03:39,719
A little mouse,
his name is Wilfred, he goes...

67
00:03:39,786 --> 00:03:41,554
"Can I get master anything?"

68
00:03:46,566 --> 00:03:48,333
-[Stephen] Is this a '64?
-[Jerry] '64.

69
00:03:48,399 --> 00:03:50,266
-I was born in '64.
-No.

70
00:03:50,333 --> 00:03:53,666
Yeah. I love how dangerous this feels.

71
00:03:53,733 --> 00:03:54,933
Watch this.

72
00:03:56,502 --> 00:03:57,770
[Stephen laughing]

73
00:03:58,966 --> 00:04:01,866
-Is that the most precious...
-And open up this,

74
00:04:01,933 --> 00:04:04,000
and Wilfred is on a little bike.

75
00:04:04,166 --> 00:04:05,333
[both laughing]

76
00:04:05,378 --> 00:04:07,647
On a chain, making them move.

77
00:04:08,181 --> 00:04:10,516
"I wasn't aware that it was spinning,
master!"

78
00:04:15,299 --> 00:04:17,399
I started off thinking
I was gonna be an actor,

79
00:04:17,466 --> 00:04:20,033
and then got hired by Second City,
and did sketch comedy,

80
00:04:20,099 --> 00:04:21,466
and cabaret for years.

81
00:04:21,966 --> 00:04:24,933
I started The Daily Show on June 2, 1997.

82
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:27,566
But that was Craig Kilborn years.
And then I left.

83
00:04:27,633 --> 00:04:29,933
Though I came back in 2000
for the election,

84
00:04:30,233 --> 00:04:34,266
and there was this new guy hosting,
Jon Stewart, who my wife knew, actually.

85
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:37,033
She always lords it over me that
she's known Jon longer than I have.

86
00:04:37,099 --> 00:04:40,099
I think his roommate dated her roommate,
or something like that.

87
00:04:40,166 --> 00:04:41,566
-Right.
-When he got the gig,

88
00:04:41,614 --> 00:04:44,484
she was like, "That's Jon Leibowitz,
what's he doing? He's not funny."

89
00:04:50,233 --> 00:04:53,933
You're one of the greatest
television performers I have ever seen.

90
00:04:54,566 --> 00:04:56,599
-Can I get out here?
-I'm just telling you that.

91
00:04:56,666 --> 00:04:59,466
-That's very nice of you to say.
-You really are. On TV.

92
00:04:59,966 --> 00:05:01,300
You had to do that.

93
00:05:01,533 --> 00:05:03,133
You had to put "on TV."

94
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:05,766
Well, I've never seen...
You're not a stage performer.

95
00:05:05,805 --> 00:05:08,041
-I was at a certain point.
-But I never saw any of that.

96
00:05:08,107 --> 00:05:09,709
-Telling you what I saw.
-No one will.

97
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:11,700
There's a lot of people on TV, Stephen.

98
00:05:11,766 --> 00:05:13,566
-A lot.
-You're very nice.

99
00:05:13,680 --> 00:05:15,481
[Jerry] What you did with that character,

100
00:05:15,548 --> 00:05:17,917
and the energy and the fun,

101
00:05:17,984 --> 00:05:20,720
-you never seemed tired.
-I was ready to stop.

102
00:05:20,866 --> 00:05:22,066
-You were?
-I was gonna stop

103
00:05:22,166 --> 00:05:23,566
-whether or not...
-Really?

104
00:05:23,623 --> 00:05:25,291
Yeah, the Letterman thing fell in my lap.

105
00:05:25,358 --> 00:05:27,894
Why'd you stop your show?
Your show was a very good show, Jerry.

106
00:05:27,966 --> 00:05:31,866
You're one of the greatest performers
I've ever seen on television. On NBC.

107
00:05:32,031 --> 00:05:34,334
One of the greatest performers
I've ever seen on NBC.

108
00:05:34,434 --> 00:05:36,102
[door handle clicking]

109
00:05:36,200 --> 00:05:39,099
-Let me reach over there...
-Not only a death trap...

110
00:05:39,266 --> 00:05:40,900
-A suicide trap.
-Yep.

111
00:05:40,966 --> 00:05:42,366
We better get out while we're young.

112
00:05:42,542 --> 00:05:45,411
[inaudible conversation]

113
00:05:49,400 --> 00:05:51,266
What time would you get home at night?

114
00:05:51,333 --> 00:05:53,833
For the first couple of years,
it was like I lived there.

115
00:05:53,966 --> 00:05:55,833
I tried to be, like, the first guy in,

116
00:05:55,888 --> 00:05:58,291
and then I wouldn't leave
till 11:00 at night.

117
00:05:58,433 --> 00:05:59,433
Wow.

118
00:05:59,666 --> 00:06:01,500
Yeah, 'cause we didn't know
what we were doing.

119
00:06:09,035 --> 00:06:10,503
Can we start with some coffee?

120
00:06:10,570 --> 00:06:11,471
-Sure.
-Thank you.

121
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:19,733
I saw your beard recently, and I thought,

122
00:06:19,799 --> 00:06:22,633
"If he hasn't shaved,
this is gonna be gruesome."

123
00:06:22,766 --> 00:06:24,066
But I see that you're...

124
00:06:24,200 --> 00:06:26,099
I'm going for a mid-career Connery.

125
00:06:26,366 --> 00:06:28,799
-A "mid-Connery," we call it.
-A mid-Connery.

126
00:06:32,166 --> 00:06:34,099
When I was in Chicago
and I'd be auditioning,

127
00:06:34,166 --> 00:06:36,500
-never got anything, by the way.
-Really?

128
00:06:36,566 --> 00:06:39,233
I think I got one Iowa Bank commercial.

129
00:06:39,333 --> 00:06:41,900
So remember these three words
for banking convenience.

130
00:06:42,033 --> 00:06:43,566
"You're first here..."

131
00:06:43,833 --> 00:06:44,866
...at FirsTier.

132
00:06:44,904 --> 00:06:46,506
[Jerry laughs]

133
00:06:46,572 --> 00:06:49,041
I like the way you adjust your glasses.

134
00:06:49,200 --> 00:06:50,500
What did I do?

135
00:06:50,700 --> 00:06:52,400
Yes. It's refined.

136
00:06:52,466 --> 00:06:54,833
It's like cleaning your pipe.
If you had a pipe,

137
00:06:54,900 --> 00:06:57,200
you could do this, you go, "Well, Jerry...

138
00:06:58,833 --> 00:07:00,433
Jerry, let me..."

139
00:07:10,333 --> 00:07:13,333
Come on, let's be honest,
a pipe and a beard is gross.

140
00:07:13,733 --> 00:07:15,566
-It's just gross.
-What, 'cause it lingers?

141
00:07:15,633 --> 00:07:18,133
Just, everything is hanging and yellow,

142
00:07:18,200 --> 00:07:20,566
the cheese, the eggs, it's all yellow.

143
00:07:20,633 --> 00:07:23,333
He's growing beards, he's packing bowls.

144
00:07:24,433 --> 00:07:27,766
The cheese. I don't think
I'm gonna have a good day today.

145
00:07:28,166 --> 00:07:30,299
Oh, my God. Oh, my God.

146
00:07:32,852 --> 00:07:34,387
Have you ever smoked a pipe?

147
00:07:34,520 --> 00:07:37,490
Uh, no, but I own several nice pipes.

148
00:07:37,599 --> 00:07:40,266
I can't believe you could
do that pipe thing

149
00:07:40,333 --> 00:07:43,299
without being a pipe smoker.
You did that so well.

150
00:07:43,366 --> 00:07:44,799
I'm sure you have talents.

151
00:07:44,866 --> 00:07:46,366
This is my biggest one.

152
00:07:46,432 --> 00:07:49,402
-Noticing? Noticing?
-Noticing what the hell's going on.

153
00:07:49,469 --> 00:07:52,605
That's very nice. My dad smoked a pipe.
My dad smoked a pipe...

154
00:07:52,700 --> 00:07:55,333
-That's where you got it from.
-He ran a hospital.

155
00:07:55,374 --> 00:07:57,677
-Ever see Hospital with George C. Scott?
-Of course.

156
00:07:57,810 --> 00:08:00,613
I would like to talk
about George C. Scott.

157
00:08:00,700 --> 00:08:01,700
George C. Scott.

158
00:08:01,766 --> 00:08:03,633
-Are we ever...
-The Flim-Flam Man.

159
00:08:03,700 --> 00:08:05,266
-...gonna see...
-Patton.

160
00:08:05,333 --> 00:08:07,266
...those guys again?

161
00:08:07,787 --> 00:08:10,857
I did a show at Second City once, where
we were doing a "men's movement" scene,

162
00:08:10,933 --> 00:08:14,366
