234
00:11:45,966 --> 00:11:47,566
It picks you up while it calms you down.

235
00:11:48,100 --> 00:11:49,833
And what's the last thing
you want to hear?

236
00:11:49,900 --> 00:11:51,633
Mendelssohn's Song Without Words,

237
00:11:51,699 --> 00:11:54,433
which is short,
two-and-a-half minutes long or something.

238
00:11:54,500 --> 00:11:56,100
Good, 'cause we don't have a lot of time.

239
00:12:00,566 --> 00:12:04,466
Do you use jazz language at all?
Like "cat," "daddy-o," "dig it"?

240
00:12:04,533 --> 00:12:06,166
I won't say "dig it" or "daddy-o."

241
00:12:06,233 --> 00:12:07,800
-"Can you dig it?"
-"Can you dig it"?

242
00:12:07,900 --> 00:12:09,300
You'll say, "Can you dig it"?

243
00:12:09,366 --> 00:12:11,100
I can say "Dig it."

244
00:12:11,433 --> 00:12:13,333
Really? You got a lot of balls.

245
00:12:21,340 --> 00:12:22,875
Did you like Whiplash?

246
00:12:23,075 --> 00:12:25,377
-Uh, you know what?
-Oh, no.

247
00:12:26,212 --> 00:12:27,880
No, don't.

248
00:12:27,947 --> 00:12:30,349
-What do you think I'm gonna say?
-I know what you're gonna say.

249
00:12:30,416 --> 00:12:32,852
You had some problems with it
and I don't wanna hear it.

250
00:12:32,918 --> 00:12:35,354
I know people who play jazz.

251
00:12:35,433 --> 00:12:38,866
They said, "The idea that
that's how you get good at jazz

252
00:12:38,933 --> 00:12:41,133
is not how you get good at jazz."

253
00:12:41,199 --> 00:12:42,533
-I know.
-And I said,

254
00:12:42,599 --> 00:12:45,300
"I've got a feeling that's not
how you get good at boxing,

255
00:12:45,366 --> 00:12:46,733
but I like Rocky."

256
00:12:54,400 --> 00:12:56,133
What do you think
of the idea for this show?

257
00:12:56,199 --> 00:12:57,433
Jerry.

258
00:12:57,766 --> 00:12:59,033
[Jerry laughs]

259
00:13:00,333 --> 00:13:01,666
How long do we get to do this?

260
00:13:01,733 --> 00:13:04,800
-Whatever you like. We can leave...
-No, no, this is very enjoyable.

261
00:13:04,866 --> 00:13:07,233
-I think the lulls are nice.
-I love the lulls.

262
00:13:07,333 --> 00:13:09,433
I can't talk as long as you can.

263
00:13:09,500 --> 00:13:12,566
You can talk in paragraphs
and just keep going.

264
00:13:12,699 --> 00:13:14,833
Let's do a lull now,
because I'm exhausted.

265
00:13:21,167 --> 00:13:23,302
-One of the--
-That was enough of a lull for you?

266
00:13:25,871 --> 00:13:28,507
Did it get uncomfortable?
Do you have to fill the silences?

267
00:13:28,574 --> 00:13:31,110
This is much longer
than you'll be on my show.

268
00:13:31,177 --> 00:13:34,914
To do a guest appearance on your show,
it's no [bleep] fun.

269
00:13:35,147 --> 00:13:36,615
-This is relaxing.
-Everything...

270
00:13:36,682 --> 00:13:38,484
-This is very relaxing.
-Exactly.

271
00:13:38,551 --> 00:13:40,953
When I come on your show,
I'm gonna do a stand-up set.

272
00:13:41,020 --> 00:13:42,955
Know how long it's gonna take
to put that together?

273
00:13:43,022 --> 00:13:45,925
So, you cannot compare the pain in the ass

274
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:50,199
of doing your show
to the joy and pleasure of doing mine.

275
00:13:53,299 --> 00:13:55,301
[inaudible]

276
00:13:59,933 --> 00:14:02,099
[Jerry] Which would you rather be,
smart or funny?

277
00:14:02,166 --> 00:14:03,766
-I'd rather be funny.
-Me, too.

278
00:14:05,666 --> 00:14:09,500
So, you'd be a clown, then?
Just an idiot in gigantic pants.

279
00:14:14,420 --> 00:14:17,489
[Jerry] Wanna go in this bookstore?
You think you're so smart.

280
00:14:23,300 --> 00:14:25,099
[Stephen] When I was younger,
living in Chicago,

281
00:14:25,166 --> 00:14:28,000
the one thing I would never deny myself
was books.

282
00:14:28,066 --> 00:14:29,633
That's really pretentious.

283
00:14:34,366 --> 00:14:35,400
Could I drive?

284
00:14:35,466 --> 00:14:37,366
-You can drive a stick?
-Sure.

285
00:14:37,443 --> 00:14:40,479
-How great do you feel with those gloves?
-They feel really nice.

286
00:14:46,051 --> 00:14:47,653
[Jerry] The weird thing, of course,

287
00:14:47,733 --> 00:14:50,300
is that New York is now
the center of talk shows.

288
00:14:50,366 --> 00:14:52,233
-Isn't that interesting?
-That's the weird thing.

289
00:14:52,300 --> 00:14:55,366
But both you and Jimmy Fallon
are adult enough

290
00:14:55,433 --> 00:14:56,933
to not get into any of that.

291
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,266
I think nothing would be more boring

292
00:14:59,333 --> 00:15:01,033
-than Late Night war.
-Yeah.

293
00:15:01,100 --> 00:15:03,068
We had drinks and talked about it,
actually.

294
00:15:03,135 --> 00:15:04,670
-You and Jimmy Fallon?
-Both drunk.

295
00:15:04,737 --> 00:15:08,274
Yeah. It was shortly after it was
announced that I was gonna get the gig.

296
00:15:08,340 --> 00:15:10,509
I said, "Let's get together
and have some drinks,"

297
00:15:10,609 --> 00:15:12,478
and we laughed and told stories.

298
00:15:12,544 --> 00:15:15,080
That's it. We just laughed like this.
And it was lovely.

299
00:15:17,433 --> 00:15:19,666
My favorite thing of today
was George C. Scott.

300
00:15:19,733 --> 00:15:22,400
-George C. Scott.
-He's not coloring his hair.

301
00:15:22,466 --> 00:15:23,900
He doesn't give a [bleep].

302
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:25,033
-Why?
-Right?

303
00:15:25,090 --> 00:15:26,959
-You think I'm coloring my hair?
-Obviously.

304
00:15:27,026 --> 00:15:27,993
I'm not.

305
00:15:42,508 --> 00:15:44,476
-That was a pretty good lull, though.
-Yeah.

306
00:15:44,733 --> 00:15:46,800
[Jerry] That was...
Might even be our best lull.

307
00:15:46,866 --> 00:15:47,699
[Stephen] Yeah.

308
00:15:52,199 --> 00:15:53,033
[gear grinding]

309
00:15:53,085 --> 00:15:55,654
Oops! Yeah. Yeah, now you're
going into first.

310
00:15:55,721 --> 00:15:57,389
-You wanna go into third.
-There.

311
00:15:58,324 --> 00:16:00,592
-I haven't driven a stick in forever.
-Really?

312
00:16:00,659 --> 00:16:02,728
Not that you can tell.
Here's why, is that I feel...

313
00:16:02,800 --> 00:16:04,166
-Oh, God.
-Sorry.

314
00:16:04,229 --> 00:16:06,932
-[gears grinding]
-No, no, no. Second... No, you...

315
00:16:06,999 --> 00:16:08,067
Wait, nothing at all?

316
00:16:08,133 --> 00:16:11,270
Oh, God, Stephen, no.
You don't know what you're doing.

317
00:16:11,370 --> 00:16:13,339
[engine racing]

318
00:16:15,366 --> 00:16:19,966
Absolutely horrible driving. Horrible.

319
00:16:20,179 --> 00:16:22,548
I'm not gonna get any action
at the end of this, am I?

320
00:16:22,648 --> 00:16:23,549
No.

321
00:16:24,133 --> 00:16:25,400
My kids aren't home.

